WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.545
Talking to Juniors.

2
00:00:01.825 --> 00:00:05.899
Day 64 of the Soft Skill Playbook course, in the Workplace phase.

3
00:00:07.649 --> 00:00:08.673
The key idea.

4
00:00:08.953 --> 00:00:11.961
Your job isn't to be the smartest person in the room.

5
00:00:12.241 --> 00:00:13.777
It's to make the room smarter.

6
00:00:14.057 --> 00:00:18.601
How you treat juniors decides whether they come to you with problems or hide them from you.

7
00:00:19.951 --> 00:00:20.805
Part 1.

8
00:00:21.085 --> 00:00:22.002
Principles.

9
00:00:23.252 --> 00:00:25.129
Psychological safety first.

10
00:00:25.409 --> 00:00:27.649
Juniors who are scared to ask hide bugs.

11
00:00:27.929 --> 00:00:29.935
Explain the why, not just the what.

12
00:00:30.215 --> 00:00:33.799
Use a transaction here because two writes must succeed together.

13
00:00:34.079 --> 00:00:35.337
Ask before telling.

14
00:00:35.617 --> 00:00:37.964
What do you think is happening? builds thinking.

15
00:00:38.244 --> 00:00:40.121
Giving the answer builds dependence.

16
00:00:40.401 --> 00:00:42.556
Praise in public, correct in private.

17
00:00:42.836 --> 00:00:43.881
Be specific.

18
00:00:44.161 --> 00:00:45.676
Good job teaches nothing.

19
00:00:45.956 --> 00:00:49.497
Your test for the null-cart case caught a real bug, nice does.

20
00:00:50.847 --> 00:00:51.722
Part 2.

21
00:00:52.002 --> 00:00:54.477
Teaching: the I do, we do, you do model.

22
00:00:55.727 --> 00:00:58.585
I do: you demo while explaining your thinking out loud.

23
00:00:58.865 --> 00:01:01.447
We do: pair programming; they drive, you guide.

24
00:01:01.727 --> 00:01:04.607
You do: they do it alone; you review afterward.

25
00:01:05.957 --> 00:01:06.853
Part 3.

26
00:01:07.133 --> 00:01:07.922
Scripts.

27
00:01:09.172 --> 00:01:10.623
Onboarding a new junior.

28
00:01:10.903 --> 00:01:11.841
You could say:

29
00:01:12.121 --> 00:01:12.911
Welcome!

30
00:01:13.191 --> 00:01:16.711
For the first two weeks, nobody expects you to ship big features.

31
00:01:16.991 --> 00:01:19.785
Your only job is to learn and ask questions.

32
00:01:20.065 --> 00:01:21.601
There are no stupid ones here.

33
00:01:21.881 --> 00:01:25.977
Let's do 15 minutes every day at 4 pm for whatever came up.

34
00:01:27.327 --> 00:01:28.586
Code-review feedback.

35
00:01:28.866 --> 00:01:31.959
Use labels so they know how serious each comment is:

36
00:01:33.309 --> 00:01:35.677
Here is a template you can copy. nit:

37
00:01:35.957 --> 00:01:40.885
(optional, style) nit: could rename d to deliveryDate for clarity. suggestion:

38
00:01:41.165 --> 00:01:46.861
(better approach) suggestion: a Map lookup would make this O(1) instead. of O(n). question:

39
00:01:47.141 --> 00:01:51.835
(curious, not a demand) question: what happens if the list is empty? blocker:

40
00:01:52.115 --> 00:01:57.128
(must fix) blocker: this SQL is built by string concat, injection risk.

41
00:01:57.408 --> 00:02:03.232
Use. a parameter. praise: praise: really clean separation of the domain logic here.

42
00:02:06.082 --> 00:02:07.853
Feedback on a bigger problem.

43
00:02:08.133 --> 00:02:09.242
Use SBI:

44
00:02:09.522 --> 00:02:12.850
Situation, then Behaviour, then Impact, then (then ask).

45
00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:15.139
You could say:

46
00:02:15.419 --> 00:02:20.560
In yesterday's release (situation), the PR was merged without tests (behaviour).

47
00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:24.616
A bug reached production and took 2 hours to roll back (impact).

48
00:02:24.896 --> 00:02:26.667
What got in the way of adding tests?

49
00:02:26.947 --> 00:02:28.397
How can I help next time?

50
00:02:29.747 --> 00:02:31.070
Delegating a task.

51
00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:32.289
You could say:

52
00:02:32.569 --> 00:02:35.214
I'd like you to own the coupon-validation endpoint.

53
00:02:35.494 --> 00:02:38.353
Goal: reject expired and over-used coupons.

54
00:02:38.633 --> 00:02:41.790
Done means: endpoint + unit tests + docs.

55
00:02:42.070 --> 00:02:42.902
Deadline:

56
00:02:43.182 --> 00:02:44.057
Thursday.

57
00:02:44.337 --> 00:02:46.107
Check-in: let's sync Tuesday.

58
00:02:46.387 --> 00:02:49.033
You decide the design; I'm here if you get stuck.

59
00:02:50.383 --> 00:02:52.004
When they're stuck but won't ask.

60
00:02:52.284 --> 00:02:53.223
You could say:

61
00:02:53.503 --> 00:02:55.380
Hey, how's the migration going?

62
00:02:55.660 --> 00:02:58.263
Getting stuck is completely normal on this one.

63
00:02:58.543 --> 00:03:00.377
It took me two days the first time.

64
00:03:00.657 --> 00:03:02.407
Want to walk me through where you are?

65
00:03:03.757 --> 00:03:05.101
When they make a mistake.

66
00:03:05.381 --> 00:03:06.319
You could say:

67
00:03:06.599 --> 00:03:08.050
This happens to everyone.

68
00:03:08.330 --> 00:03:10.911
I once took down staging for a whole afternoon.

69
00:03:11.191 --> 00:03:14.434
Let's fix it together first, then talk about how to prevent it.

70
00:03:15.784 --> 00:03:17.917
Saying no to their idea (kindly).

71
00:03:18.197 --> 00:03:19.136
You could say:

72
00:03:19.416 --> 00:03:21.549
I like that you're thinking about performance.

73
00:03:21.829 --> 00:03:27.547
Right now, the bottleneck is the database, not the JSON parsing, so a rewrite wouldn't help much.

74
00:03:27.827 --> 00:03:30.493
Can you profile it and show me where the time goes?

75
00:03:30.773 --> 00:03:33.056
If the data says otherwise, let's do it.

76
00:03:34.406 --> 00:03:35.323
Part 4.

77
00:03:35.603 --> 00:03:36.905
Mistakes to avoid.

78
00:03:38.155 --> 00:03:39.008
Avoid:

79
00:03:39.288 --> 00:03:42.189
This is easy, just do X (it isn't easy for them).

80
00:03:42.469 --> 00:03:43.323
Avoid:

81
00:03:43.603 --> 00:03:45.971
Taking the keyboard and fixing it yourself.

82
00:03:46.251 --> 00:03:47.104
Avoid:

83
00:03:47.384 --> 00:03:49.069
Sarcasm in code reviews.

84
00:03:49.349 --> 00:03:50.203
Avoid:

85
00:03:50.483 --> 00:03:52.893
Only giving feedback when something goes wrong.

86
00:03:53.173 --> 00:03:54.027
Avoid:

87
00:03:54.307 --> 00:03:57.251
Answering in jargon without checking they understand.

88
00:03:58.601 --> 00:03:59.561
Part 5.

89
00:03:59.841 --> 00:04:01.953
Questions to ask juniors regularly.

90
00:04:03.203 --> 00:04:04.888
What's one thing you learned this week?

91
00:04:05.168 --> 00:04:08.304
What's confusing about our codebase that nobody explains?

92
00:04:08.584 --> 00:04:10.205
What would you like to work on next?

93
00:04:10.485 --> 00:04:12.341
How can I be a better mentor for you?

94
00:04:13.691 --> 00:04:14.630
Part 6.

95
00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:16.297
Practice conversation:

96
00:04:16.577 --> 00:04:18.347
Feedback on a junior's PR.

97
00:04:19.597 --> 00:04:21.624
Here is how the conversation goes.

98
00:04:21.904 --> 00:04:24.528
Hi Kavya, I've reviewed your PR.

99
00:04:24.808 --> 00:04:26.323
Have you got ten minutes?

100
00:04:26.603 --> 00:04:27.456
Yes.

101
00:04:27.736 --> 00:04:29.037
Is it bad?

102
00:04:29.317 --> 00:04:30.512
Not at all.

103
00:04:30.792 --> 00:04:33.117
The logic is clean and easy to read.

104
00:04:33.397 --> 00:04:35.808
There are two things to fix before we merge.

105
00:04:36.088 --> 00:04:37.197
Okay.

106
00:04:38.547 --> 00:04:40.147
The conversation continues.

107
00:04:40.427 --> 00:04:43.286
First, there are no tests for the empty-cart case.

108
00:04:43.566 --> 00:04:46.425
What do you think happens if the cart is empty?

109
00:04:46.705 --> 00:04:48.283
Oh... it might throw an error.

110
00:04:48.563 --> 00:04:49.609
Exactly.

111
00:04:49.889 --> 00:04:53.707
And second, the SQL is built with string concatenation.

112
00:04:53.987 --> 00:04:56.185
Can you use a parameter instead?

113
00:04:56.465 --> 00:04:58.193
Yes, I'll fix both today.

114
00:04:59.543 --> 00:05:01.143
The conversation continues.

115
00:05:01.423 --> 00:05:02.169
Great.

116
00:05:02.449 --> 00:05:05.372
Ping me when it's ready, and I'll approve it quickly.

117
00:05:06.722 --> 00:05:08.258
That's the end of this lesson.

118
00:05:08.538 --> 00:05:13.189
Pick one script from it, say it out loud three times, and use it for real today.

119
00:05:13.469 --> 00:05:19.591
For extra practice, paste the Master Prompt into an AI assistant and role-play the conversation.

